.TH CTI-AGENT-UPDATE 1 "2026-11-08" "cti-agent 1.5" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
cti-agent-update \- bring the fleet box up to date: pull the kit, then run the installer from it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo cti\-agent\-update
.RB ( \-\-dry\-run | \-\-for\-real )
.PP
.B sudo /root/cti\-agent\-kit/fleet\-kit/update\-fedora.sh
.RB ( \-\-dry\-run | \-\-for\-real )
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cti-agent-update
is the whole update of a fleet box. It fast-forwards the
.I kit
checkout (the one
.B install\-fedora.sh
was run from, normally
.IR /root/cti\-agent\-kit ),
then hands over to the installer from the freshly pulled kit.
.PP
There are two checkouts on the box. The kit holds the installer, the runners,
the units, the command manifest and these man pages. The installer pulls the
other one, the Go source under
.IR /opt/cti\-agent/agent ,
itself. Nothing else pulls the kit, so running the installer alone would run
last release's installer over last release's files and build this release's
source. Pulling the kit first is the reason this command exists.
.PP
.B cti-agent-update
is a three-line script that
.B install\-fedora.sh
writes to
.IR /usr/local/sbin ,
which is on sudo's secure_path. It runs
.B fleet\-kit/update\-fedora.sh
in the kit it was installed from. Before the first install, run that file by
its path instead.
.PP
On
.BR \-\-for\-real ,
in order:
.TP
.B 1. Checks
Refuses to run from inside
.I /opt/cti\-agent
(the tree the installer pulls), without root, outside a git checkout, when
the kit has local changes to tracked files, or when its branch has no
upstream.
.TP
.B 2. Fetch
.B git fetch \-\-tags
on the kit. The tags come along because the installer stamps the version
from the nearest tag. If the fetch fails, git's own message is shown and
nothing is pulled. A tag git will not move ("would clobber existing tag")
means the upstream's history was rewritten; see
.BR "REWRITTEN HISTORY" .
.TP
.B 3. Fast-forward
.B git merge \-\-ff\-only
to the upstream, if anything is incoming. A kit that has diverged from its
upstream is refused, never merged, with the same advice as a moved tag.
.TP
.B 4. Install
Prints the kit's version before and after, with a note when the new one is
not an exact release tag (for example
.BR 1.5.1\-2\-gabc1234 ,
which usually means a tag was not pushed). It then
.BR exec s
.B install\-fedora.sh \-\-for\-real
from the updated kit (the installer has no default either: run by hand it needs
.B \-\-dry\-run
or
.BR \-\-for\-real ). That keeps
.IR fleet.env ,
the other files in
.I /etc/cti\-agent
and the state in
.IR /var/lib/cti\-agent ,
and enables no timer.
.SH HOW TO RUN
\fBcti-agent-update\fR changes the install, so it has no default: it has to be told how to run. Give exactly one of
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR (fetch and show what is incoming, change nothing) or \fB\-\-for\-real\fR (pull the kit and install from it).
With neither it prints a usage message and exits 2 without doing anything, and
both together are refused the same way. This is the rule for every fleet
command that changes something.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Fetch, then print the kit's path, its current version, the upstream's
version and the number of incoming commits, with their one-line log and a
diff summary. Nothing is merged and nothing is installed: an installer run
from the old checkout would describe the old install, not the update. Does
not need root, but a kit under
.I /root
does.
.TP
.B \-\-for\-real
Pull the kit and run the installer from it. Needs root. When the kit is
already up to date, the installer is still run.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage line and exit 0. Nothing is fetched.
.PP
.B \-\-uninstall
and
.B \-\-purge
are refused (exit 2): they belong to
.BR install\-fedora.sh ,
so run that for them. Any other argument is refused the same way, before
anything is fetched, so a typo never leaves the kit pulled and the install
not run. The installer is always run with no arguments.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FLEET_UPDATE_UID
For the tests only: stands in for
.B id \-u
in the root check.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/sbin/cti\-agent\-update
This command, written by the installer with the kit's path in it.
.TP
.I /root/cti\-agent\-kit/fleet\-kit/update\-fedora.sh
What it runs. The kit's location is wherever it was cloned for the first
install.
.TP
.I /etc/cti\-agent/version
What the last install recorded.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Dry run finished, or
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B 1
Refused: not root for
.BR \-\-for\-real ,
the kit is not a git checkout, has local changes, has no upstream, could not
be fetched, or cannot be fast-forwarded. Nothing was pulled, and the reason
is printed.
.TP
.B 2
Usage: no mode, both modes,
.B \-\-uninstall
or
.BR \-\-purge ,
an unknown argument, or run from inside
.IR /opt/cti\-agent .
.PP
After the hand-over on
.BR \-\-for\-real ,
the exit status is the installer's. If the installer stops partway, it
prints the line it stopped on and that the box is half-installed.
.SH REWRITTEN HISTORY
After the upstream's history is rewritten on purpose (for example by
.IR scripts/scrub\-history.sh )
and force-pushed, both checkouts on the box still hold the old commits and
tags: the kit, and the source the installer pulls in
.IR /opt/cti\-agent/agent .
A fetch will not move their tags and a fast-forward cannot reach the new
branch, so the update stops and prints the one-time fix: check the kit has
no local changes, then for each checkout
.B git fetch \-\-force \-\-tags origin
and
.BR "git reset \-\-hard @{u}" ,
then a dry run. It is printed, never run: a reset discards what the checkout
holds. If you did not rewrite the history, find out who did first.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sudo cti\-agent\-update \-\-dry\-run     # what is incoming; changes nothing
sudo cti\-agent\-update \-\-for\-real    # pull the kit, then install
sudo cti\-agent cti\-mailer \-\-check | head \-2   # afterwards: what the binary says it is
sudo cat /etc/cti\-agent/version                # what the install recorded
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cti\-agent (1),
.BR fleet\-timers (1),
.BR git\-merge (1)
.PP
RUNBOOK.md section 7; CHEATSHEET.md, "Updating the box"; CHANGELOG.md, "Finding out what is
deployed".
